<div class="story-grid">
<article class="article" aria-label="Moses Malone statistical investigation">
<h2 id="moses"><span class="section-index">01 / THE MAN BEHIND THE NUMBERS</span>He didn't wait for the ball to come to him</h2>
<p class="lead">Moses Malone built a Hall of Fame career by treating a missed shot as a beginning, not the end of a possession.</p>
<p>Long before the NBA became a league of positionless creators and seven-foot shooters, Malone was one of the sport's great specialists in a deceptively difficult skill: winning the ball back after somebody missed it. The first shot might belong to a guard. The rebound was his. And once he had it, the possession often became a fight at the rim.</p>
<p>Born in Petersburg, Virginia, he entered the pros directly from high school in 1974, signing with the ABA's Utah Stars when most future NBA players still took the college route. Over the next two decades, through the ABA and NBA, he became a relentless worker on the glass, an interior scorer and a player whose physical persistence changed games. By the end of his career, he had earned three NBA MVP awards.</p>
<p>He wasn't just tall or strong. Malone had an unusual understanding of rebounding angles, timing and where an attempt was likely to land. He often chased his own misses, absorbed contact and created a second or third opportunity while a defender was still trying to complete the first stop. The effect rarely fits into the tidy idea of a 'go-to move'. His go-to move was refusing to let an offensive possession die.</p>
<p>In 1982, Philadelphia traded for him after falling short against the Lakers in the previous Finals. The 76ers already had Julius Erving, Andrew Toney, Maurice Cheeks and Bobby Jones. They didn't need a new offensive identity as much as they needed someone to solve the problem under the basket. Malone gave them exactly that.</p>

<h2 id="season"><span class="section-index">02 / THE HISTORIC SEASON</span>The year of “Fo', Fo', Fo'”</h2>
<p>In <strong>1982–83</strong>, Malone averaged <strong>24.5 points, 15.3 rebounds and 2.0 blocks per game</strong> over 78 regular-season appearances. His total rebounds led the NBA, and he collected <strong>5.7 offensive rebounds per game</strong> alone. For context, that is almost six extra possessions or immediate scoring chances created from misses every night, though an offensive rebound does not always start a separate recorded possession.</p>
<p>Those figures weren't empty accumulation. Philadelphia went <strong>65–17</strong>. Malone won his third league MVP, made All-NBA First Team and All-Defensive First Team, and became the central inside presence of the eventual champions. His true shooting was <strong>57.8%</strong>, compared with the NBA's approximately <strong>53.1%</strong> that season: a positive <strong>4.7 percentage-point</strong> efficiency margin.</p>
<p>Before the postseason, Malone famously predicted three straight sweeps: “Fo', Fo', Fo'.” The Sixers nearly delivered. They swept the Knicks, beat Milwaukee in five games, then swept Kareem Abdul-Jabbar and the Lakers in the Finals. The result was a <strong>12–1 postseason</strong> and a Finals MVP for Malone, who averaged roughly 26 points and 18 rebounds against Los Angeles. The prediction didn't need to be exact to become legendary.</p>
<blockquote>His defining talent wasn't only scoring from the paint. It was making the defense guard the same possession again.<small>Anticipate / The offensive glass</small></blockquote>
<p>That context matters because today's NBA can produce a center with similar points, usage and rebounding percentages while asking him to take three-pointers, facilitate from the perimeter or screen in a different geometry of the court. The numbers might rhyme while the jobs do not.</p>

<h2 id="methodology"><span class="section-index">03 / THE INVESTIGATION</span>How we searched for another Moses</h2>
<p>Our question was deliberately narrower than “who is as good as Moses Malone?” We asked which qualified modern player's measurable <strong>2025–26 regular-season profile</strong> most closely resembles his 1982–83 season. This investigation uses the same <strong>279-player dataset</strong> and model as the previous four NBA Time Machine stories. Every player in the modern pool logged at least 1,000 minutes. Players who were traded have one combined-season row.</p>
<p>The model compares <strong>seven statistical measures</strong>: points per 100 possessions, true shooting relative to league average, assist percentage, total rebound percentage, usage, steal percentage and block percentage. It also considers listed height and weight and a broad guard/forward/center designation.</p>
<p>Every numeric difference is standardized using the spread of the 279 modern players. Then three groups combine into a distance score: <strong>70% statistics, 20% physical profile and 10% position</strong>. Smaller distance means a closer match, not a higher similarity percentage. Three-point accuracy and free-throw shooting are present in the underlying dataset but don't affect this model's score.</p>
<div class="research-unit">
<span class="smallcaps">The method, without the mystery</span>
<p class="formula">distance = √(0.70 × statistical distance² + 0.20 × physical distance² + 0.10 × position penalty)</p>
<p>Inside the statistical group, we average the squared standardized differences across the seven metrics; inside the physical group, across height and weight. A position match has penalty 0; a mismatch has penalty 1. The published results and data live in the <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/anticipate-nba-time-machine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open Anticipate model repository</a>.</p>
</div>
<p>One important boundary: the model tracks <em>total</em> rebound percentage, not offensive rebound percentage specifically. It has no direct measure of box-out technique, putbacks, contested boards, post-up frequency or extra possessions created. That gap is especially important for Moses Malone, whose offensive rebounding was his trademark.</p>
<p>Listed measurements are only approximate cross-era proxies. Our historical reference file uses 208 cm and 215 lb for Malone; contemporary official profiles don't always agree about his listed height. The difference between 1980s body listings and modern rosters should not be mistaken for precise biometric evidence.</p>

<h2 id="result"><span class="section-index">04 / THE RESULT</span>The closest match was a stretch five</h2>
<p>The winner was <strong>Karl-Anthony Towns</strong>. Not because our model thinks he is the same kind of player as Moses. Towns ranks first because several key ingredients of the 1982–83 season line up remarkably well: overall scoring volume, usage, league-adjusted efficiency and total rebound share. The output is less absurd when you see what the model is actually measuring.</p>
</article>
