<h2 id="towns"><span class="section-index">06 / THE BASKETBALL ARGUMENT</span>Karl-Anthony Towns wins the math. Jalen Duren has a basketball case.</h2>
<h3>Why Towns is actually first</h3>
<p>It starts with a pair of numbers that are almost identical. Malone used <strong>26.0%</strong> of his team's plays while he was on the floor; Towns used <strong>25.9%</strong>. Malone grabbed <strong>21.6%</strong> of available rebounds; Towns grabbed <strong>21.4%</strong>. Even the scoring and efficiency are close: <strong>30.5 vs. 32.1 points per 100 possessions</strong> and <strong>+4.7 vs. +3.8 percentage points</strong> of true shooting above their respective league averages.</p>
<p>That is an impressive overlap in four of the model's seven statistical dimensions. It captures a high-usage, high-production big man who is not merely present on the glass but one of the team's main finishers. Towns is a legitimate modern player to examine for that <em>overall production footprint</em>.</p>
<p>But if you close your eyes and picture a possession, the resemblance gets weaker. Towns is comfortable spacing the floor, attacking from the perimeter and shooting threes. He made <strong>36.8% of his threes</strong> in the 2025–26 data used here, attempting about <strong>6.6 per 100 possessions</strong>. Malone's 1982–83 offense did not work that way. Almost all of his offensive value was created inside, at the foul line and by recovering missed shots. Towns also had a substantially larger assist share: 14.7% to Moses's 5.1%.</p>
<p>So the model finds similarity in <strong>how much</strong> they contributed to scoring, rebounding and possession usage. It does not establish similarity in <strong>how</strong> they produced those contributions. That is the difference between an accurate statistical answer and a careless comparison.</p>
<h3>Why Duren might look more like Moses on film</h3>
<p>Jalen Duren is second at a distance of <strong>0.6795</strong>. His rebounding share, <strong>20.9%</strong>, sits very close to Malone's <strong>21.6%</strong>. His block rate is exactly <strong>3.0%</strong> in our dataset, matching Malone's. He plays a much more interior-focused brand of basketball than Towns, with most of his threat generated near the basket and through physical contact.</p>
<p>He also isn't a perfect mirror. Duren's 2025–26 relative true-shooting figure of <strong>+10.7 percentage points</strong> is far above Moses's +4.7. His listed 250 lb differs significantly from the historical model's 215 lb. And even if Duren is the more satisfying stylistic parallel, neither modern player's numbers tell us that he shares Malone's elite offensive-rebound anticipation, persistence or title-level impact.</p>
<blockquote>Towns reproduces the statistical outline. Duren offers more of the interior vocabulary.<small>Anticipate / Production versus style</small></blockquote>
<p>Both observations can be true, and together they are more revealing than pretending the first-ranked name settles the argument.</p>

