<h2 id="other"><span class="section-index">07 / THE OTHER THREE</span>What the rest of the top five reveal</h2>
<h3>Kel'el Ware: rebounding without Malone's offensive burden</h3>
<p>Ware is third at <strong>0.7054</strong>. His <strong>21.1% rebound share</strong> is remarkably close to Moses's, and his assist percentage of <strong>4.6%</strong> looks more like a traditional low-assist center's profile than Towns's 14.7%. His size and rim protection also fit. Yet he recorded only <strong>23.2 points per 100 possessions</strong> on <strong>18.0% usage</strong>. He did not shoulder the offensive workload that made Malone a league MVP. His perimeter shooting adds another stylistic split.</p>
<h3>Jarrett Allen: the traditional-center argument</h3>
<p>Allen is fourth at <strong>0.7214</strong>. A rolling, finishing, paint-oriented center with credible rim protection, he may pass the eye test more quickly than Towns in several half-court actions. He doesn't pass the production test nearly as closely. His <strong>17.2% total rebound rate</strong> and <strong>19.9% usage</strong> are meaningfully below Malone's 21.6% and 26.0%. Familiar role, different volume.</p>
<h3>Jalen Smith: why context still matters</h3>
<p>Smith rounds out the top five at <strong>0.8029</strong>. He meets our threshold with 1,095 minutes, but that is far less court time than the other highlighted names, and his 19.0% usage and 23.1 points per 100 are distant from the 1983 MVP's output. A player can enter the model's top five without being a convincing historical replica. In fact, ranking fifth is useful only when we explain that limitation.</p>

<h2 id="sensitivity"><span class="section-index">08 / TEST THE ASSUMPTIONS</span>Change the weights. Change the winner.</h2>
<p>Because the model assigns editorial weights, we should never present its single ranking as an inevitable truth. We recalculated the distances for all 279 modern players under two alternative settings. The exercise exposes how much any “closest match” depends on what we think matters.</p>
