<p><strong>The important shift:</strong> when we make the model more about height, weight and positional group, <strong>Kel'el Ware</strong> moves to number one. Under production-first weights, <strong>Karl-Anthony Towns</strong> stays first, with Duren still second. This doesn't make Ware the “real” next Moses either. It shows the conclusion depends on which version of resemblance we're asking for.</p>

<h2 id="verdict"><span class="section-index">09 / THE VERDICT</span>You can match a season without matching a player</h2>
<p>The official answer to the model's question is Karl-Anthony Towns. His scoring, usage, total rebound share and era-adjusted efficiency line up surprisingly well with Moses Malone's best championship season. It is a fair, reproducible statistical match from the eligible 2025–26 pool.</p>
<p>The basketball argument is less settled. Duren offers a more familiar interior-oriented game; Ware has the rebounding share and center profile but not the scoring responsibility; Allen captures some traditional big-man actions but not Malone's volume. All of them overlap with part of Moses. None reproduces his particular combination of offensive rebounding obsession, interior pressure, physical endurance and championship dominance.</p>
<p>There are also limits built into the dataset. We compare one season from each player. We ignore coaching assignments, playoff value and teammates; we don't score offensive rebounding separately, despite its central importance here; and historical listed weights can be inconsistent. Changes in the three-point line, spacing and offensive tactics make direct era-to-era comparisons imperfect even after adjusting true shooting for league average.</p>
<p>For Moses Malone, perhaps the fairest answer is that the modern NBA has plenty of talented big men but very few who make the entire game revolve around the offensive rebound. <strong>Towns is the best answer to the model. Duren is a fascinating answer to the basketball question. Moses remains the standard.</strong></p>

